  <section class="border-t border-ink-800/50 py-12">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 prose prose-invert">

      <h2>Studio licensing &amp; inspections</h2>
      <p>In most U.S. states, tattoo studios must hold a license, and inspectors check the things that prevent outbreaks: sterilization records, a per-client record for every person tattooed, reporting of infections or adverse reactions, and the conditions under which a minor can be tattooed. Texas, for example, sets those minor-consent conditions under <a href="https://www.dshs.texas.gov/tattoo-body-piercing-studios/licensing-requirements-tattoo-body-piercing-studios" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent-400 hover:underline">25 TAC §229.406</a>.</p>
      <p><strong>What you can do:</strong> a current license should be displayed. If you don't see one, ask. A studio that can't show its license is telling you something.</p>

      <h2>Bloodborne-pathogen controls (OSHA 29 CFR 1910.1030)</h2>
      <p>Needles and ink are blood-exposed, so U.S. workplaces — including tattoo studios — fall under OSHA's Bloodborne Pathogens standard. It requires a <strong>written Exposure Control Plan reviewed at least annually</strong>, rules for handling and disposing of contaminated sharps, a <strong>sharps injury log</strong>, and <strong>training at initial assignment and at least every year after</strong>. The point of the plan is that safety stops relying on anyone's memory.</p>
      <p>Read the standard directly: <a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.1030" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent-400 hover:underline">29 CFR 1910.1030 — Bloodborne Pathogens</a>.</p>

      <h2>Standard precautions (CDC)</h2>
      <p>The CDC's <a href="https://www.cdc.gov/infection-control/hcp/basics/standard-precautions.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent-400 hover:underline">Standard Precautions</a> are the baseline for any setting where blood or body fluid exposure is possible. The core elements are straightforward: hand hygiene, personal protective equipment whenever exposure is expected, safe injection and sharps handling, and cleaning and disinfection of equipment and surfaces between clients.</p>

      <h2>Ink safety (FDA)</h2>
      <p>The U.S. FDA regulates tattoo inks as <strong>cosmetics</strong>. That matters because contaminated inks have triggered recalls in <strong>2004, 2012, and 2017</strong>, plus a <strong>2019 safety advisory</strong> — and the FDA notes infections have resulted from contaminated ink <em>even when the artist followed hygienic procedure</em>. In other words, clean needle technique is necessary but not sufficient; the ink itself has to be clean.</p>
      <p>Source: <a href="https://www.fda.gov/cosmetics/cosmetic-products/tattoos-permanent-makeup-fact-sheet" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent-400 hover:underline">FDA — Tattoos &amp; Permanent Makeup: Fact Sheet</a>.</p>
      <p><strong>What you can do:</strong> ink should come in a fresh, single-use cap opened in front of you. Be wary of "custom mixes" poured from unlabeled bottles you can't trace.</p>

      <h2>EU rules (Regulation (EU) 2020/2081)</h2>
      <p>In the European Union, tattoo inks and permanent make-up are covered by <a href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2020/2081/oj" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent-400 hover:underline">Commission Regulation (EU) 2020/2081</a>, which <strong>restricts a listed set of substances from 4 January 2022</strong> and requires mandatory pack labelling from the same date. Two pigments — Pigment Blue 15:3 and Pigment Green 7 — were given a limited derogation until <strong>4 January 2023</strong>. This is a restriction on specific substances, not a ban on tattoo ink.</p>
      <p>If you're getting tattooed in the EU, you can ask whether the inks in use are REACH-compliant and labelled accordingly.</p>

      <h2>What to check before you sit</h2>
      <p>You don't need to be a regulator to spot a safe studio. Run this checklist:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>License:</strong> current and displayed.</li>
        <li><strong>Needles:</strong> from sealed, single-use packaging, opened in front of you.</li>
        <li><strong>Sterilization:</strong> an autoclave with validation; tubes and jewellery are either single-use or autoclaved.</li>
        <li><strong>Ink:</strong> a fresh ink cap per client, no double-dipping.</li>
        <li><strong>Gloves:</strong> worn by the artist and changed between tasks.</li>
        <li><strong>Surfaces:</strong> cleaned and disinfected between clients.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Healing is the last link in safety</h2>
      <p>A clean procedure still needs clean aftercare. What you do in the first 48 hours decides a lot of how it heals — see our <a href="/blog/tattoo-aftercare-guide" class="text-accent-400 hover:underline">tattoo aftercare guide</a> for the day-by-day plan.</p>

      <h2>About this guide</h2>
      <p>
        Written by <strong>Sarah Chen</strong>, Founder &amp; CEO at InkFlow, with 12 years running multi-artist tattoo studios. Sourced from primary regulations: <a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.1030" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent-400 hover:underline">OSHA 29 CFR 1910.1030</a>, the <a href="https://www.fda.gov/cosmetics/cosmetic-products/tattoos-permanent-makeup-fact-sheet" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent-400 hover:underline">FDA Tattoos &amp; Permanent Makeup Fact Sheet</a>, <a href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2020/2081/oj" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent-400 hover:underline">Commission Regulation (EU) 2020/2081 (EUR-Lex)</a>, the <a href="https://www.cdc.gov/infection-control/hcp/basics/standard-precautions.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent-400 hover:underline">CDC Standard Precautions</a>, and the <a href="https://www.dshs.texas.gov/tattoo-body-piercing-studios/licensing-requirements-tattoo-body-piercing-studios" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent-400 hover:underline">Texas DSHS studio licensing requirements</a>. Published August 4, 2026.
      </p>

      <h2>FAQ</h2>
      <h3>Are tattoo inks regulated by the FDA?</h3>
      <p>Yes — the FDA regulates tattoo inks as cosmetics. Contaminated inks have led to recalls in 2004, 2012, and 2017 and a 2019 safety advisory, and infections have occurred from contaminated ink even with hygienic technique.</p>
      <h3>What does OSHA require of tattoo studios?</h3>
      <p>The Bloodborne Pathogens standard (29 CFR 1910.1030): a written Exposure Control Plan reviewed at least yearly, safe sharps handling and disposal, a sharps injury log, and training at hire and at least annually.</p>
      <h3>Is tattoo ink banned in the EU?</h3>
      <p>No. Regulation (EU) 2020/2081 restricts specific listed substances in tattoo inks from 4 January 2022 and requires pack labelling; it is a restriction, not a ban. Pigment Blue 15:3 and Pigment Green 7 had a limited derogation to 4 January 2023.</p>
      <h3>How do I know a studio is safe before I book?</h3>
      <p>Look for a current license on display, needles from sealed single-use packaging opened in front of you, an autoclave with validation, fresh ink caps per client, and gloved artists who change gloves between tasks.</p>
      <h3>Can a tattoo get infected even if the artist is careful?</h3>
      <p>Yes. The FDA notes infections have resulted from contaminated ink even when the artist followed hygienic procedure — which is why ink sourcing and single-use caps matter as much as needle hygiene.</p>
